  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "The equation $Ax^3+Bx^2+D=0$, where $A$, $B$ and $D$ are non-zero, has a double root.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the double root is $-\\dfrac{2B}{3A}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The double root is $-\\dfrac{2B}{3A}$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=Ax^3+Bx^2+D$. A double root satisfies $P(x)=0$ and $P'(x)=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P'(x)&=3Ax^2+2Bx\\\\0&=x(3Ax+2B)\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x=0$ or $x=-\\dfrac{2B}{3A}$. Since $P(0)=D\\ne0$, the double root is $-\\dfrac{2B}{3A}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 31
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence show that $27A^2D+4B^3=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$27A^2D+4B^3=0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P\\left(-\\frac{2B}{3A}\\right)&=A\\left(-\\frac{2B}{3A}\\right)^3+B\\left(-\\frac{2B}{3A}\\right)^2+D\\\\0&=-\\frac{8B^3}{27A^2}+\\frac{4B^3}{9A^2}+D\\\\0&=\\frac{4B^3}{27A^2}+D\\\\0&=4B^3+27A^2D\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $27A^2D+4B^3=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      }
    ]
  },
